      "serie": "Hors série",
      "pitch": "# The Roar of Two Kingdoms\n*An original Lion Guard story — audiobook pitch (private, personal use)*\n\n**Logline:** Years after the Tree of Life, King Kion must answer to two kingdoms at once. A pride born of drought is uniting the Outlands, and the only way to save both homes is the power he swore never to misuse: the Roar of the Elders.\n\n**Hook:** Kion has a crown, his queen Rani, and a scar that still burns when he's angry. Kiara rules the Pride Lands with Kovu. When the rains fail in both realms, a new lioness, Zuri-Ama, gives starving Outsiders a home in the old volcano. War looms with Kion's own family on opposite sides, and the original Guard reunites for one last patrol.\n\n## Why fans will love it\n- The original Guard reunites.\n- Kion and Kiara rule side by side.\n- Ono's eyesight gets a heroic payoff.\n- It answers whether the scar's venom is really gone, and bridges to the Lion King II timeline.\n- Rani, Makini, Janja and Fuli all get real arcs.\n\n## Cast\nKion, Rani, Kiara, Kovu, Bunga, Fuli, Beshte, Ono, Makini, Janja, Rafiki and Mufasa's spirit.\nNew: **Zuri-Ama**, an antagonist you end up feeling for, and **Tamu**, Rani's young cousin.\n\n## Tone\nA warm adventure for ages 8 and up, with grown-up themes of duty, grief and forgiveness.\n\n## 14 chapters\n1. Dry Season\n2. Message from Pride Rock\n3. The Volcano Pride\n4. Old Friends\n5. Janja's Warning\n6. Split Council\n7. Riverbed Ambush\n8. Through Ono's Eyes\n9. The Scar Wakes\n10. Mufasa in the Clouds\n11. Two Kingdoms March\n12. Zuri-Ama's Truth\n13. The Gentle Roar — Kion calls the storm instead of the army.\n14. Rain on Both Kingdoms — Tamu joins the next Guard.\n\n## Length\nAbout 2 h 30, around 23,000 words (10–11 minutes per chapter).\n\n## Narration\nOne warm Arena voice with light character voices: Bunga talks fast, Fuli is clipped, Beshte is slow, Zuri-Ama is low and kind. Savanna ambience between chapters; drums only for the Roar scenes. No Disney songs.\n",
